{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"apps/clax_app/timing_lint/lib/timing_lint.dart","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T18:59:46.745227Z","id":"6b72cc9dc4b57ed069f35f39c25f95bf787917f290a2cd4cf61542f7db105455","body":{"detail":"`realTime` (line 161) holds only for a file under integration_test/ or a call written inside a function literal passed to `runAsync`. Only a `testWidgets` body runs in a fake-async zone: a `test(...)` body (184 files under apps/clax_app/test call `test(`) and the support code it calls run on real time, so `await Future.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 1))` there is a real one-second sleep that the rule passes. apps/clax_app/timing_lint/test/timing_lint_test.dart:43-46 pins exactly that, as 'a delay in fake time', for a bare async function. One instance stands today: apps/clax_app/test/support/hosted_daemon.dart:114 waits a real 20 ms between polls of a real daemon with no exemption, while the same kind of poll interval at apps/clax_app/integration_test/rig.dart:271-272 has to carry `// ignore: timing_delay`. `Timer(d, ...)` and `Timer.periodic(d, ...)` are named by no rule in either directory (apps/clax_app/test/core/scheme_load_real_test.dart:38 starts a real 1 ms periodic timer unflagged). docs/usage.md:704-705 states the rule as real `Future.delayed` \"inside `runAsync`, or anywhere in `integration_test/`\", which leaves the plain tests out.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":161},"end":{"line":168},"content_hash":"806f9ba2b8c31af77148d5f9a6a23f61532d9811d93d31ddb722129765988fa6"},"suggested_fix":"Take a non-zero `Future.delayed` as fake time only when it is written inside a `testWidgets` callback and outside `runAsync`, or inside a `Clock`; flag it elsewhere under test/, with `// ignore: timing_delay -- <why>` for a helper that only ever runs in a fake zone. Add `Timer` and `Timer.periodic` with a non-zero duration to the same rule, give both a case in timing_lint_test.dart, and exempt hosted_daemon.dart:114 with its reason.","summary":"timing_delay takes every delay in test/ outside a runAsync closure as fake time, but a plain test() body and the helpers it calls run on the real event loop","tags":["review","review:deterministic-tests-2026-11-12","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"apps/clax_app/timing_lint/lib/timing_lint.dart","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-12T19:07:32.283584Z","id":"866a3f0cf16cc352273c9366bf0459afd9283c0f53b9cee83d173150e67f5b25","body":{"detail":"apps/clax_app/timing_lint/lib/timing_lint.dart:161 is `final realTime = integration || _inRunAsync(node);` and lines 164-168 report `timing_delay` only when `realTime && d != Duration.zero && !_inClock(node)`. `integration` is `path.split('/').contains('integration_test')` (line 77) and _inRunAsync (lines 310-319) walks up to a function literal passed to a call named `runAsync`. Nothing asks whether the enclosing test is a `testWidgets`.\n\napps/clax_app/timing_lint/test/timing_lint_test.dart:43-46 expects no finding for `f() async { await Future.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 1)); }` under the name 'a delay in fake time'.\n\nThe instance: apps/clax_app/test/support/hosted_daemon.dart:114 is `await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 20));` inside the `waitUntil` closure that polls a real daemon's daemon.json and /healthz (lines 100-116), with no `// ignore:` on it or the line before. Its one caller, apps/clax_app/test/remote/push_pull_real_test.dart:27-30, is a plain `test(` whose body awaits `HostedDaemon.start()`; that file has no `testWidgets` and no `runAsync`. The same kind of poll interval at apps/clax_app/integration_test/rig.dart:271-272 carries `// ignore: timing_delay -- the poll interval of a condition wait under a hang guard`. bin/test_timing.dart:21-28 reads every .dart file under test/ and integration_test/, so hosted_daemon.dart is linted and passes.\n\n`grep -n 'Timer' apps/clax_app/timing_lint/lib/timing_lint.dart` prints nothing: no rule names `Timer` or `Timer.periodic`. apps/clax_app/test/core/scheme_load_real_test.dart:38 is `final tick = Timer.periodic(const Duration(milliseconds: 1), (_) {`, in a helper a plain `test(` (line 67) calls.\n\ndocs/usage.md:704-705 states the rule as real `Future.delayed` and `pump(d)` \"(inside `runAsync`, or anywhere in `integration_test/`)\".\n\n`grep -rlE '(^|[^A-Za-z_])test\\(' apps/clax_app/test --include=*.dart | wc -l` prints 185, one more than the 184 the finding gives; the difference changes nothing.\n\nThe kind is right: a hole in the rule with one real 20 ms poll through it, not a failing test.","kind":"comment","references":"6b72cc9dc4b57ed069f35f39c25f95bf787917f290a2cd4cf61542f7db105455","summary":"confirmed: under test/ a non-zero Future.delayed is flagged only inside a runAsync closure, and a plain test() runs one on real time unflagged","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:82c42a10-6c82-42ed-a424-ae8ec2a814c7"]}}
